1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,800
Hi, today we will look at aperture
and what it does

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,900
Aperture is the opening inside your
lens, it lets light in

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:10,000
A low number like f-one-point-eight
means a wide opening

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,100
A wide opening gives you a blurry
background behind your subject

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,200
A high number like f-eleven means a
narrow opening

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:19,300
A narrow opening keeps more of the
scene sharp and in focus

7
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:22,400
I shoot portraits at
f-two-point-eight most of the time

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,500
For a group photo I move to
f-five-point-six or higher

9
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:26,999
Watch your shutter speed, a wide
aperture lets in more light

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,299
Try the same shot at f-two, f-four,
and f-eight today

11
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:31,200
If the photo is too bright, close the
aperture one stop

12
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,300
Compare the three shots and look at
the background blur

13
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,400
The background blur is called bokeh,
it is not magic

14
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,500
Your lens matters too, a cheap lens
can still teach you a lot

15
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,600
Do not buy a new lens until you test
the one you have

16
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:46,700
Set the camera to aperture priority
and take ten test shots

17
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:49,800
Check the edges of each shot, not
just the middle

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,900
Write down the setting for your
favourite shot of the day

19
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:56,000
Tomorrow we will look at shutter
speed and how it freezes motion

20
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,100
Thanks for watching, try it today and
tell me how it went

